This doesn't have the `end > line` check! So if `end == line` (empty line), then `*(end-1)` reads `line[-1]` which is OUT OF BOUNDS (before the buffer). But wait, that's a READ before the buffer, not after. And the issue is "heap-buffer-overflow READ 1" which usually means reading past the end.

Hmm, but actually, let me reconsider. When is `end == line`? `end = line + strlen(line)`. If the line is empty (just `\n`), then `strlen(line) == 1` (the `\n`), so `end = line + 1`. So `end != line`. 

